
VIDEO 3 SCRIPT

Connect Supabase and Store Real Data (7 Minutes)

Title:
Connect a Real Database to Your Next.js App (Supabase in 7 Minutes)

⸻

0:00 – FULL FACE (Camera Large)

Say:

“In the last video, we ran our app locally.”

Pause.

“Now we’re connecting a real database and saving actual data.”

Pause.

“No authentication. No complexity. Just one table. One insert. One read.”

Pause.

“Let’s make it real.”

Hard cut.

⸻

0:25 – FULL SCREEN SHARE

Show the working local app from Episode 2.

Say:

“This is running locally on my machine.”

Pause.

“Right now, it doesn’t store anything permanently.”

Pause.

“We’re going to change that.”

⸻

PART 1 – Create Supabase Project

0:50

Open: supabase.com

Click “New Project”.

Say:

“Supabase is a hosted PostgreSQL database. It gives us a real backend without managing servers.”

Keep it simple.

Create project.

Wait for it to initialize.

⸻

1:30 – Get Project Credentials

Open Project Settings → API.

Copy:
	•	Project URL
	•	anon public key

Say:

“These two values allow our frontend to talk to the database.”

Pause.

“We’ll store them safely as environment variables.”

⸻

PART 2 – Add Environment Variables

2:00

Open local project.

Open .env.local.

Add:

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=your_url_here
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your_key_here

Say:

“Environment variables are configuration values. They prevent us from hardcoding sensitive data.”

Pause.

Save file.

Restart dev server if needed.

⸻

PART 3 – Create Database Table

2:40

Switch back to Supabase.

Open SQL Editor.

Paste:

create table notes (
  id uuid primary key default gen_random_uuid(),
  content text not null,
  created_at timestamp with time zone default now()
);

Say:

“This creates a simple table called notes.”

Pause.

“One column for content. One timestamp. One ID.”

Click Run.

⸻

PART 4 – Install Supabase Client

3:20

Open terminal.

Run:

npm install @supabase/supabase-js

Say:

“This installs the Supabase client so our app can communicate with the database.”

Let it finish.

⸻

PART 5 – Create Supabase Client File

3:50

Create file: lib/supabaseClient.ts

Add:

import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js'

export const supabase = createClient(
  process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL!,
  process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY!
)

Say:

“This initializes the connection using our environment variables.”

Pause.

⸻

PART 6 – Add Simple Insert + Read Logic

4:20

Open homepage file.

Replace content with minimal example:

'use client'

import { useEffect, useState } from 'react'
import { supabase } from '@/lib/supabaseClient'

export default function Home() {
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState<any[]>([])
  const [input, setInput] = useState('')

  async function loadNotes() {
    const { data } = await supabase
      .from('notes')
      .select('*')
      .order('created_at', { ascending: false })
    setNotes(data || [])
  }

  async function addNote() {
    if (!input) return
    await supabase.from('notes').insert({ content: input })
    setInput('')
    loadNotes()
  }

  useEffect(() => {
    loadNotes()
  }, [])

  return (
    <main style={{ padding: 40 }}>
      <h1>Database Connected</h1>
      <input
        value={input}
        onChange={(e) => setInput(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Write something"
      />
      <button onClick={addNote}>Save</button>

      <ul>
        {notes.map((note) => (
          <li key={note.id}>{note.content}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </main>
  )
}

Say:

“This is minimal. One input. One button. One list.”

Pause.

“We insert. Then we read.”

Save file.

⸻

PART 7 – Test It

5:40

Refresh browser.

Type:

“First real data”

